<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · concept</p><h1 class="enc-title">Wassail</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as wassailing, wasselling</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Wassail is an English custom of Christmastide and the hot drink of mulled cider, ale or wine and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spice-trade">spices</a> that goes with it. The word was borrowed from the Old Norse salutation ves heill, 'be in good health'; its earliest record as a drinking formula is of around 1140, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geoffrey-of-monmouth">Geoffrey of Monmouth</a>, and by c. 1300 it also meant the drink itself. In the house-visiting wassail, traditionally on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/twelfth-night-holiday">Twelfth Night</a> (5 January), people go from door to door singing and offering a drink from the wassail bowl in exchange for gifts, a practice largely displaced by carol singing. In the orchard-visiting wassail of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>'s cider-producing regions, people sing to the trees to promote a good harvest.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/wassail.jpg" alt="Wassail" width="330" height="440" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Joss Smithson · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Shirehampton_wassail_music.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>&quot;Here We Come A-wassailing&quot;, also known as &quot;Here We Come A-Caroling&quot;, &quot;Here We Come A-Christmasing&quot;, &quot;<b>Wassail</b> Song&quot; and by many other names, is a traditional English Christmas carol and New Year song.</blockquote><a href="/songs/here-we-come-a-wassailing">Here We Come A-wassailing</a></li></ul></section>
